# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This page describes how we work together as the Integration Hub team. It is not
a rulebook. It is a shared agreement that helps each of us do our best work,
and keeps us aligned in how we deliver, whether the work is easy or not.

## What matters to us

* **Kindness and trust**: we assume good intent and are generous with our time
  and patience.
* **Openness**: we share what we are learning and make our decisions easy to
  find.
* **Quality**: we take pride in building things that are secure, reliable and
  easy for others to understand.
* **A sustainable pace**: we work in a way we can keep up, so we can keep doing
  good work.

## Working together

We turn cameras on by default in team meetings. Seeing each other makes
conversation easier and helps us feel like a team.

We keep our Slack status and calendars up to date, so people can plan work
without having to guess who is around. Nobody is expected to reply outside
their working hours or while their status says they are away.

When a conversation, meeting or huddle leads to a decision, action or useful
learning, write it down where the people affected can find it.

## Looking after ourselves and each other

Delivering well matters, but not at the expense of anyone's wellbeing. If you
are feeling overloaded, stuck or unwell, tell someone. We would much rather
change the plan than have someone struggle quietly.

Take your breaks and your leave, and switch off at the end of your day.

### Learning from mistakes

Things will sometimes go wrong. When they do, we focus on understanding what
happened and improving how we work. Raising a mistake early is always welcome:
it helps us fix things sooner and learn together.

### Learning and development

The organisation offers 10% of our time for learning and development, and we
want everyone to use it. It is not a reward for finishing other work first. It
is part of how we grow as individuals and as a team. Plan it in, let the team
know what you are exploring and share anything useful you learn.

### Celebrating wins

We take time to notice progress, big and small: a feature shipped, a tricky
problem solved or a colleague who went out of their way to help. Share your
thanks in Slack, at stand-up or in a retrospective.

## Slack

Slack is where most of our day-to-day collaboration happens.

* Use `#integration-hub-team` for team discussion, coordination and, sharing
  what we are learning.
* Use `#ask-integration-hub` for questions and requests from people using, or
  planning to use, our services.

Slack is asynchronous. If you need an answer by a particular time, say when and
why, so people can prioritise.

We do our best to acknowledge customer questions during working hours and let
people know what will happen next. The Integration Hub is not yet a live
service, so this will develop with our customers' needs.

Use threads to keep channels easy to follow. Never share passwords, private
keys, file contents or other sensitive data in Slack.

## GitHub

GitHub is where our code, CI/CD pipelines and product backlog live. If work
needs an owner, a decision or a useful history, it belongs in GitHub.

When something is blocked, say so on the issue. When it is resolved, close the
loop where people are already following the work. Nobody should have to piece
together a decision from half a dozen old Slack threads and a meeting they did
not attend.

### Pull requests and reviews

A good pull request helps the reviewer understand the change quickly. Explain
what it changes and why, how you checked it, and link to the issue,
architecture decision record or discussion behind it. Smaller pull requests
are easier to review well.

Every pull request is reviewed by a team member other than its author.
Reviewing is everyone's job: if you see a pull request waiting, pick it up
rather than waiting for someone else to. Be kind and specific in your comments,
and ask when you do not understand something.

Once a pull request is approved, its author will generally merge it, so the
person with the most context sees the change through.

### Our repositories

* [Integration Hub](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub)
